A1. Nothing could be sweeter than this. ~ HONEY
A4. This fellow's wife was a pillar in the community. ~ LOT
A6. The sirens of today. (Plural) ~ VAMPS
A9. This is old stuff. ~ AGE
A11. What a married man holds his wife in. ~ AWE
A13. A preposition meaning "in direction of." ~ TO
A14. Passes on—communicates. ~ TRANSMITS
A15. Innocent little pronoun that "went wrong." ~ IT
A17. Past tense of verb which means "to perform." ~ DID
A18. This adjective pertains to the past. ~ AGO
A20. What the old hen did. ~ LAID
A23. What a good boy never does. ~ TELLS
A25. This doesn't amount to much in a chess game. ~ PAWN
A27. Boxing glove for a game rooster. ~ SPUR
A29. To repent of. ~ RUE
A30. To restrain. ~ BATE
A31. How the Fairy Prince found the Princess. ~ ASLEEP
A33. What night clubs sometimes get—for a while. ~ BARRED
A35. There are a lot of kicks in this. ~ FOLLIES
A36. What the first Americans did on the long, long trail. ~ CAMPED
A39. How the lights of Honeymoon Lane are burning. ~ DIMMED
A42. Two of a kind. ~ PAIR
A43. To recede. ~ EBB
A45. Parking place for petters. ~ NOOK
A46. To portion out. ~ METE
A47. What a Prohibitionist thinks he is. ~ DRYER
A49. What the poorest bank-roll consists of. ~ ONES
A51. What a straight-shooter must have. ~ AIM
A52. Only people with taking ways can do this successfully. ~ ROB
A54. In like manner or degree. ~ AS
A55. These kind of things are easily stirred up. ~ EXCITABLE
A56. A personal pronoun. ~ ME
A58. A poetic even. ~ EEN
A59. A wet party couldn't get much of a kick out of this. ~ TEA
A61. What anything does during a decline. ~ SINKS
A62. A ram's storm and strife. ~ EWE
A63. A fluke. ~ BONER

D1. This is a very roomy place. ~ HOTEL
D2. Feminine affirmative. ~ NO
D3. This has more feet than a man and less than a horse. ~ YARD
D4. What a pawn broker is. (No profanity). ~ LENDER
D5. This is hot stuff. ~ TAMALE
D6. A "no" that means something. ~ VETO
D7. A possessive pronoun. ~ MY
D8. This is smooth as silk. ~ SATIN
D10. You can tell a sailor by this. ~ GAIT
D12. This is over the heads of some old-timers. ~ WIGS
D16. The only kind of men who can talk chorus-girls into dining at Childs. ~ DIPLOMATS
D19. Pastime for Peggy Hopkins, etc. ~ MATRIMONY
D21. This fellow is continually making a donkey of himself. ~ ASS
D22. What all shall receive on Judgment Day. ~ DUE
D24. A sleep inducer—not a black-jack. ~ LULLABY
D25. Equality; equal footing. ~ PAR
D26. A little thing in Scotland. ~ WEE
D28. One way to "pass the buck." ~ REFER
D30. Used to cleanse the complexion. (Almost extinct) ~ BASIN
D31. This is found in all circles. ~ ARC
D32. Where the best peas come from. ~ POD
D33. This has always been the bunk in Judge's Crossword Puzzles. ~ BED
D34. This man had at least one little trouble. ~ DAD
D37. A progenitor outlawed in Tennessee. ~ APE
D38. When this gets half-baked it's enough to make any one sick. ~ PIE
D40. The call of the discontented cow. ~ MOO
D41. To just get by. ~ EKE
D43. What cuties like to get wrapped up in. ~ ERMINE
D44. What the fred man would do. ~ BERATE
D46. What poets often do without. ~ MEALS
D47. Bones you should never dig up. ~ DICE
D48. You could get all wrapped up in this. ~ ROBE
D50. This is bull. ~ STEER
D51. These have an edge on. ~ AXES
D53. Words—and nothing else. ~ BLAB
D57. Where instalment-collectors should never find you. ~ IN
D60. Above and touching—a preposition. ~ ON


